WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.634
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:05.100
I take a look at some tools to
test Google Cloud Platform's

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.060
networking performance.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.892
Will we find the
perfect multitasker?

00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:08.850
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.442 --> 00:00:12.900
Over the past few
weeks, we've been

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.970
peppered with all sorts
of performance questions

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.590
centered around
networking and how

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.540
to configure GCP to get
the best possible results.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.410
Now we've got a couple of videos
talking about some developers

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:23.620
that we were able to help.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.500
But before that, I
wanted to take a moment

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.780
and talk about some of the
tools available to profile

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.510
GCP's networking so that you
don't have to write that code

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.380
yourself.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.140
First is Netperf, which
is a commonly used tool

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.590
for measuring maximum TCP and
UDP bandwidth performance.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.260
Netperf works by hosting
a server and a client

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.660
on different machines and
can measure the throughput

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.640
between the two ends,
either unidirectionally

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.751
or bidirectionally.

00:00:45.751 --> 00:00:48.000
When you execute the client,
it connects to the server

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.670
and sends a boat
load of data around.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.400
Now, once you install
Netperf, the server version,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.170
called Netserver, will
be run automagically.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.680
So you just need to pick which
instance will be the client

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.200
and execute the Netperf
binary to get the details

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.330
on the bandwidth conditions.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.880
Iperf is a TCP
and UDP benchmark,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.180
and has several
advantages over Netperf,

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.660
like bandwidth shaping
and threaded support

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.410
so that multiple
streams can be created.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.970
Getting Iperf working is
somewhat straightforward.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.120
Simply install Iperf on each
of your target instances

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.120
and run the server on one
and the client on the other.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.950
What we get is an
output from Iperf

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.280
is a timed stamped report of
the amount of data transferred

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.870
and the throughput measure.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.610
Now while Iperf
and net stats can

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.410
help you track latency
and throughput,

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.410
the Traceroute tool is useful
for measuring and displaying

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.582
the route that packets
take across the network.

00:01:36.582 --> 00:01:38.040
It records the
history of the route

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.150
as well as a roundtrip time
of three packets received

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.465
from each successive
host in the route.

00:01:43.465 --> 00:01:45.090
If you'd like to see
this for yourself,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.150
run Traceroute on
your home machine.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.990
Here's my result,
when you can see

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.870
that the first hop to the
ISP is significantly slow

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.645
given our distance
from that service.

00:01:53.645 --> 00:01:55.020
Meanwhile, check
out the distance

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.740
between a VM running on Google
Compute Engine and google.com.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:01:59.850
Much less hops.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.260
Now once you have
the tools, it's

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.030
important to address
another issue--

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.920
how to properly test things.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.230
This can be a tricky
problem since setting up

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.750
the wrong tests can lead to
inaccurate results, which

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.150
isn't something you want to
be making decisions off of.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.360
This is exactly why Google
maintains the PerfKit

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.820
Benchmarker project.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.480
Its authors have done
the due diligence

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.310
to set up their testing
harness correctly

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.364
and reduce the inserted error
that might skew the results.

00:02:23.364 --> 00:02:25.530
So if you're serious about
testing your performance,

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.720
it's worth playing around with.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.344
Now if you're interested in
a few more details, including

00:02:30.344 --> 00:02:32.760
some gotchas with the firewalls
to get these tools working

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.650
on Compute Engine,
check out the article

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.756
for this video in the
description below.

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.880
And if you want to get more
great Cloud Performance

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.590
content, check out
the rest of our videos

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.100
because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.141
every millisecond counts.

